Simplify: Let's create script as per reference but only fill Config, State, Reset parts.

We need to ensure code does not cause errors: For placeholder sections we can leave empty comment lines.

Define startLoop stub as placeholder that does nothing, to avoid errors later if code tries to call startLoop? Not yet. But later tasks may reference startLoop, but they will also create it. Could cause duplication errors (function already defined). That would be a problem.

Thus better to leave function definitions for later tasks as they will add them. But if they will later add startLoop but see function already defined, they'd error. So we should not define placeholders for functions that will be defined later. Instead, leave these sections empty (or comment placeholder) but not define any functions.

However the boot area will call reset() and startLoop? The reference boot only calls reset() which itself triggers startLoop within reset. In our code, we can just call reset() for now (no startLoop). That's safe.

Thus our script will only define constants, state, reset function, and call reset. No other functions defined.

